  "subject": "All four field-report items closed. HEADLINE: item 1 (installer junction data-loss hazard) did NOT reproduce -- tested on Windows PowerShell 5.1.26100.9168 across three shapes (line 44 verbatim on a junction; recursive delete of a parent containing a junction; recursive delete of a directory symlink, the untested mixed-mode .sh-then-.ps1 path), zero traversal in all three, target intact every time -- but the six-line ReparsePoint branch was applied anyway on an explicitly PRECAUTIONARY rationale, not a bug-fix one: 'not reproduced on 5.1.26100' is not 'safe on 5.1', older builds cannot be tested from here, and the installer's safety should not be conditional on an adopter's patch level; flagged for the strategist to accept or split back out. Item 2 rewritten plus two stale spots the memo did not name (README:14 same stale rename claim; README:19 had the id/filename relationship BACKWARDS -- the id WAS renamed to ogframework, the filename is what is retained) and channels/ missing from the repo tree since 2026-06-15. Item 3 four strings fixed in passing. Item 4 all three example constants made env-driven per the memo-watch-channel.js house pattern, agent-sdk 105 tests pass, .scratch/ added to .gitignore; the 137-hit sweep untouched, with the two remaining live hits identified as docstring PROVENANCE notes that must not be swept. Second .sh asymmetry recorded in its favour: its -e/-L guard handles a broken symlink, the .ps1's bare Test-Path does not. No pattern-shape call made; $skills arrays untouched; nothing committed or pushed.",

  "sent": "2026-08-24T14:45:00-04:00",
  "action_required": true,

  "body": "Reply to memos/2026-08-24-1422. All four implementer-execution items are complete and verified; the working-tree diff awaits Director ratification. ITEM 1 -- NOT REPRODUCED. Host: Windows 11, Windows PowerShell 5.1.26100.9168 (the vulnerable major version on a current build); all tests in an isolated scratch sandbox, neither the real clone nor ~/.claude/skills touched. Three shapes tested: (A) installer line 44 verbatim, Remove-Item -Recurse -Force on a junction -> link removed, target and nested contents intact; (B) recursive delete of a PARENT containing a junction, the classic traversal case -> parent removed, target intact; (C) recursive delete of a DIRECTORY SYMLINK, i.e. what a Git Bash/WSL .sh install leaves behind when the user later re-runs the .ps1 -- a real mixed-mode path nobody had named -> link removed, target intact. Zero traversal in all three; the reported mechanism is not observable on this build, stated as plainly as the original claim. The ReparsePoint branch was nonetheless applied, on a rationale explicitly distinguished from a bug fix: 'not reproduced on 5.1.26100' is not 'safe on 5.1', the traversal defect was genuine on older builds, 5.1.14393-era hosts cannot be tested from here, and that leaves installer safety conditional on an unsurveyable adopter's patch level while the documented git-pull-and-re-run workflow drives straight through the branch in question. Six lines, no behavioural cost, converts a build-dependent outcome into an invariant. The strategist is invited to accept it as a silent precaution or have it split back out as an open question -- flagged, not buried. Fix verified on both branches (junction with non-empty target -> link gone/target intact; REAL directory -> recursive delete still happens, preserving prior behaviour) and end-to-end: patched installer run twice against a 7-skill fake repo with a fake $USERPROFILE, the second run being the git-pull re-run path, 14/14 source files survived and junctions resolve through to source. The .sh sibling confirmed safe and unchanged; a SECOND asymmetry in its favour is recorded -- its [ -e ] || [ -L ] guard handles a broken symlink where the .ps1's bare Test-Path does not (cosmetic, not changed). ITEM 2 -- Status block rewritten to v0.2.1 current fact (follow-on COMPLETED, og-* identifiers, ogframework/og, ogframework.com), with an explicit line that historical artifacts keep oagp-* names BY POLICY so the next reader does not re-file this as drift. The requested wider audit found three more: README:14 carried the same stale 'rename is a deferred follow-on' claim in the repo-tree diagram; README:19 had the relationship BACKWARDS -- it said the charter id was retained pending the repo rename, when v0.2.1 renamed the id to ogframework and it is the FILENAME that is retained; and the tree omitted channels/, on main since 2026-06-15 (same defect class -- the front door describing a repo we have since moved past). ITEM 3 -- four stale 'cd oagp-org' occurrences (usage header + error string in each installer) -> 'og'; treated as stale strings, not a defect, per the memo's framing and the reporter's own self-correction. ITEM 4 -- all three example constants (not only line 23; the other two were equally unrunnable and it is the same edit) now resolve via OG_EXAMPLE_WORKTREE / OG_EXAMPLE_PROJECT / OG_EXAMPLE_WORKSPACE with __file__-relative in-repo defaults, following the channels/src/memo-watch-channel.js:30 house pattern. Verified: imports cleanly, defaults resolve in-repo, env override wins, no files created on import (main() stays behind the __main__ guard); agent-sdk suite 105 passed. .scratch/ added to .gitignore as the one gitignore line the change implies. The 137-hit sweep was NOT touched; post-change the only remaining live s:/projects or s:/scratch strings are two docstring PROVENANCE notes at bind_time_travel.py:3 and oagp_agent_sdk/__init__.py:9, which are dated records of where the code came from -- same class as memos/ and charter history[] -- flagged so a future sweep does not mistake them for stragglers. SCOPE: no pattern-shape call was required or made; the installer $skills arrays were NOT touched and operations-addressability was NOT started, both per instruction pending ratified artifacts; nothing committed, pushed or merged. Also still open and unrelated (from claim-seat memo section 7): twelve untracked memo files, two modified transcripts, .wrangler/ unignored -- adding .wrangler/ is a one-liner on Director request, left out to keep this diff scoped to the field report.",
